# Response headers for the Cloudflare Workers asset bundle (assets directory =
# ./static, wrangler.jsonc).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The SPA has always carried these policies as
# <meta http-equiv=…> tags, and a meta tag cannot deliver two of them: browsers
# ignore X-Frame-Options in a meta (the console says so on every page load), and
# CSP refuses frame-ancestors in a meta-delivered policy. So the clickjacking
# protection the pages appeared to declare has never been in force. Delivered as
# headers they take effect, and they now also cover admin.html, logistics.html
# and transporter.html, which declare nothing at all.
#
# WHY EVERYTHING IS IN ONE BLOCK. A per-path block is not honoured by this
# platform: with a CSP declared for / and /index.html, the deployed site sent the
# universal headers on /index.html and no CSP at all — measured 2026-10-23 at
# 19:36Z against agribrige.com and agribridge.zealmugumya.workers.dev, both HIT.
# So a path-scoped CSP cannot be shipped this way, and a CSP on /* would be worse
# than useless: admin.html, logistics.html and transporter.html declare no CSP and
# have never been measured against one, so a blanket policy would silently
# re-policy the screens the owner runs the business from. The meta CSP in
# index.html keeps enforcing script/style/connect there, as it already did.
#
# Cloudflare's own analytics beacon is blocked by that meta policy. Whitelisting
# https://static.cloudflareinsights.com would enable visitor tracking, which is a
# product decision, not a fix, so it is left alone.
#
# X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN is what the pages already declared; it is not a
# stricter policy than the site believed it was running.

/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains
